dialogue => [
['You (New Joiner)', q{(before the SteerCo prep) <strong>Boundary questions first, Daniel:</strong> <em>do you want me silent, or is thinking-aloud welcome? And where do you want my hands — own keyboard or yours?</em> Five seconds of asking saves an hour of guessing.}, q{（SteerCo 准备会前）<strong>先问边界问题，Daniel：</strong><em>你要我沉默，还是欢迎我出声思考？我的手放哪——你键盘还是我键盘？</em>问五秒钟，省一小时瞎猜。}],
['Daniel B (Senior Dev)', q{<strong>Thinking aloud welcome, my keyboard.</strong> <em>You are the navigator — questions, not driving.</em> If you see me about to break something, <strong>interrupt loudly.</strong> The best pairing question I ever got was "why not the naive approach first?" — <em>ask me that at least once.</em>}, q{<strong>欢迎出声思考，键盘归我。</strong><em>你是领航员——提问，不开车。</em>如果你看到我要弄坏什么，<strong>大声打断。</strong>我收到过最好的结Pair问题是"为什么不先试朴素方案？"——<em>至少问我一次。</em>}],
['You (New Joiner)', q{(during) <strong>Navigator question, the naive one:</strong> why are we writing the retry logic before measuring whether retries even happen? <em>Is that measurable in yesterday\'s logs?</em>}, q{（过程中）<strong>领航员问题，朴素那条：</strong>为什么在测量"重试是否真的发生"之前就写重试逻辑？<em>昨天的日志里能测吗？</em>}],
['Daniel B (Senior Dev)', q{…because — <em>huh.</em> It is measurable. <strong>Forty-seven retries in yesterday\'s batch.</strong> Okay, you just earned us an hour: we write the counter first, ship the retry only if the number justifies it. <strong>That is navigator gold</strong> — note it for the retro.}, q{……因为——<em>咦。</em>可以测。<strong>昨天的批次里有 47 次重试。</strong>好，你为我们赚了一个小时：先写计数器，数字值得才上重试。<strong>这是领航员的金子</strong>——记下来给回顾会用。}],
['You (New Joiner)', q{(after, SteerCo shadow debrief) <strong>Shadowing debrief, one page as promised.</strong> Three things I watched for: <em>how Sarah opens with the ask, how she handles the CFO\'s interruption, and what she does NOT present</em> — three slides she skipped because the audience had them already. <strong>The skipped slides were the lesson.</strong>}, q{（会后，SteerCo 旁听复盘）<strong>旁听复盘，如约一页纸。</strong>三个观察点：<em>Sarah 如何以请求开场、如何应对 CFO 的打断、以及她不呈现什么</em>——她跳过了三张幻灯片，因为听众早已掌握。<strong>被跳过的幻灯片才是课程。</strong>}],
['Sarah (Manager)', q{<strong>"The skipped slides were the lesson"</strong> — that is the sharpest debrief line I have read from a new joiner.</em> One question back: <em>what would you have cut that I did not?</em>}, q{<strong>"被跳过的幻灯片才是课程"——这是我从新人那里读过最锋利的复盘句。</em>回敬一个问题：<em>有什么是我没砍而你认为该砍的？</em>}],
['You (New Joiner)', q{<strong>Slide six — the risk table.</strong> You narrated it while it was already on screen; <em>the room reads tables faster than you can say them.</em> I would have paused there and asked for questions instead. <strong>But that is a week-three opinion from someone who has never faced a CFO</strong> — I am holding it loosely.}, q{<strong>第六页——风险表。</strong>你念它的时候它已经在屏幕上了；<em>房间读表格的速度比你念得快。</em>我会在那里暂停、改为征求提问。<strong>但这是第三周的意见，来自一个没面对过 CFO 的人</strong>——我持保留态度地持有它。}],
['Sarah (Manager)', q{Held loosely, noted seriously — <em>that combination is rare and welcome.</em> Next SteerCo, you take slide six.}, q{保留态度地持有、严肃地记下——<em>这个组合难得且受欢迎。</em>下次 SteerCo，第六页归你讲。}],
