grammar => [
['The probe as three slots', q{<em>"Which moment specifically did the job?"</em> — the probe grammar: <strong>which [noun] specifically [verb]?</strong> which moment worked, which line confused, which slide dragged. The word <strong>specifically</strong> does the mining — it converts adjectives into situations. Ask it immediately after any praise; the freshness is the ore.}, q{"Which moment specifically did the job?"——探针语法：<strong>which [名词] specifically [动词]？</strong>哪个瞬间起效、哪句令人困惑、哪页拖沓。<strong>specifically</strong> 一词完成挖掘——把形容词变成情境。在任何夸奖后立刻使用；新鲜度就是矿藏。}],
['Hypothetical critique', q{<em>"If you were running that walkthrough, what would you have cut?"</em> — second conditional as a kindness: the <strong>if-you-were-me frame</strong> lets the reviewer critique a hypothetical version instead of your reality. Same information, half the sting — and, as Priya proved, twice the honesty.}, q{"If you were running that walkthrough, what would you have cut?"——第二条件句作为善意：<strong>if-you-were-me 框架</strong>让评审者批评一个假想版本而非你的现实。信息相同、刺痛减半——而且如 Priya 所证，诚实加倍。}],
],
tips => [
q{<strong>Never archive praise as a trophy.</strong> "Great job" is raw ore — mine it the same day, while the moment is fresh.},
q{<strong>One probe per compliment.</strong> "Which moment specifically did the job?" — the answer is a habit with a name you can repeat on purpose.},
q{<strong>Ask the mirror question.</strong> "What would you cut?" unlocks honesty that direct critique never carries.},
q{<strong>Name the sting, list the action.</strong> "Both sting productively" keeps the conversation warm AND convertible.},
q{<strong>Retire the disclaimer.</strong> "As a new joiner" discounts work that should stand on its own — the work is the credential.},
],
practice => [
{ t => 'Mine your last compliment', p => 'Take the last "great job" you received. Run the probe: which moment, what action, what effect? Then write the habit with a name.', a => q{Name format: "the risk-slide pause" — situation (risk slide), action (stop talking), effect (the room starts thinking). A habit with a name is teachable, repeatable, and yours.} },
{ t => 'Mirror question practice', p => 'Someone presents to you next week. Prepare your version of "what would you cut?" plus one probe question for their strongest moment.', a => q{Asking both directions — what worked, what to cut — makes you the colleague people present to early. The probe-and-mirror pair is the complete feedback instrument; most people carry neither.} },
{ t => 'Disclaimer audit', p => 'Search your last three presentations for disclaimers: "as a new joiner", "I might be wrong", "just a suggestion". Rewrite each sentence without the disclaimer.', a => q{The rewrite test: does the sentence stand on its evidence? "This might be wrong, but the join double-counts" becomes "The join double-counts — the reactivation rows appear twice in the appendix." Evidence replaced apology; if the evidence is weak, fix the evidence, not the sentence.} },
],
